    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284630": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I - Astronomy",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - Astronomy | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - Astronomy Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Extended Site Maintenance We are performing updates and maintenance to our applicant experience. As a result, the site will be unavailable Saturday, October 31st at 9pm EST through Sunday, November 1st at 8:00am EST. During this outage period, applications for job postings can not be submitted. Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Please also include the contact information for two (2) individuals who have agreed to provide recommendations if needed.  Review of applications will begin Monday, November 9, and we expect to extend an offer no later than Monday, November 16, 2026.  This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Job Summary The Department of Natural Sciences at UM-Dearborn is seeking applications for one or more LEO Lecturer I non-tenure track appointments for the Winter 2027 term, for which classes begin on January 6, 2027.  The contractual full-time rate per term is $25,500.00, salary is dependent upon the number of courses taught, up to 33.33% appointment for the Winter term. The LEO Lecturer I would be teaching one Astronomy lecture course (ASTR 133) at the undergraduate level for the Winter 2027 Semester. ASTR 133 is a four-credit lecture course, meeting twice per week,  in-person, on the Dearborn campus. Classes in the 2027 Winter semester will run from January 6 - April 20, with final examinations taking place during the interval from Friday, April 23 - Thursday, April 29.  Based on performance and enrollment, the LEO Lecturer I may be renewed for subsequent semesters. Responsibilities* Duties of this position include teaching an undergraduate lecture course with full-course responsibility. This includes providing students a syllabus and materials using the online course management system; evaluating and grading students; setting and grading examinations including the final examination; holding regularly scheduled office hours; conducting engaging and informative class sessions; ensuring students are informed of any class developments; and handling routine matters related to instruction and assessment. Required Qualifications* Candidates must possess an earned Masters degree in Astronomy or Physics, with relevant teaching experience and evidence of teaching excellence. Desired Qualifications* Prefer candidates who have earned a PhD in Physics or Astronomy or a related professional subject, with relevant specialization. Why Work at U-M Dearborn? The University of Michigan-Dearborn (UM-Dearborn) is one of the three campuses of the University of Michigan. UM-Dearborn, a comprehensive university offering high quality undergraduate, graduate, professional and continuing education to residents of southeastern Michigan, and attracts more than 9,000 students. Our faculty comes from respected universities and doctoral programs, are recognized for excellence in research and teaching, and are active in professional and academic service roles in their respective fields. US News and World Report recently recognized our campus as a Best Regional University. The campus is located on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate. Dearborn is centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The geographically diverse area provides faculty with a variety of urban, suburban, and rural areas within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, Detroit suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284630 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - Astronomy Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Physical Sciences Posting Begin/End Date 10/21/2026 - 11/04/2026 Salary $25,500.00 - $25,500.00 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/285247": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I - ANTHROPOLOGY",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - ANTHROPOLOGY | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - ANTHROPOLOGY Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should upload a CV, and may optionally include a brief letter of introduction, indicating relevant teaching and/or research experience or other information. Candidates may be asked for additional information as the search progresses. Deadline to apply is November 13 , 2026. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Who We Are The University of Michigan-Dearborn (UM-Dearborn) is one of the three campuses of the University of Michigan. UM-Dearborn, a comprehensive university offering high quality undergraduate, graduate, professional and continuing education to residents of southeastern Michigan, and attracts more than 9,000 students. Our faculty comes from respected universities and doctoral programs, are recognized for excellence in research and teaching, and are active in professional and academic service roles in their respective fields. US News and World Report recently recognized our campus as a Best Regional University. The campus is located on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate. Dearborn is centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The geographically diverse area provides faculty with a variety of urban, suburban, and rural areas within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, Detroit suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Job Summary The Anthropology Program invites applicants for a Lecturer I, non-tenure-track appointment which begins January 1, 2027, through April 30, 2027 for the WINTER 2027 term. Contractual full-time rate per term is $25,500 per term. S alary is dependent on number of courses taught and course effort percentage, up to 33.33% appointment for the WINTER 2027 term. The position is for one section of a four-field Introduction to Anthropology (ANTH 101) taught 100% in person to dual enrollment (high school) students. The class will take place at Grosse Ile High School and is scheduled for two days per week 8am-9:20am. The course description is as follows: Anthropology emphasizes the holistic study of human beings, in both the past and the present, and this course introduces students to the four primary subfields of the discipline (sociocultural anthropology, biological anthropology, linguistic anthropology, and archaeology) of the discipline. This course shows students how the sub-fields intersect to explain human biological and cultural diversity, provides students with the ability to better understand their own culture in the context of a globalized world, and discusses the applied skills of the discipline. Depending on enrollments, the ideal candidate may also be considered to teach one or more sections of the following courses to regular university students, either online or on the UM Dearborn campus: ANTH 376: Power and Privilege in Southeast Michigan (an Academic Service Learning, community-based course), ANTH 421: Education and Culture, ANTH 406: Sexuality and Culture, ANTH 425: Language and Culture, ANTH 371: African Experience in the Americas. Course descriptions can be found in the online catalog (https://catalog.umd.umich.edu/undergraduate/coursesaz/anth/) . A mix of 100% online, hybrid, and 100% in person formats can be considered, and classes vary between 3 and 4 credits. Most are offered to a mix of Anthropology students and those from other majors. Experience teaching and researching in the topics of these courses and prior teaching experience is strongly preferred. Experience working with area nonprofits, government, or corporations on applied anthropology or related projects also preferred. Required Qualifications* A PhD degree in Anthropology is required in hand, with experience teaching and/or researching in areas of anthropology related to the course. Desired Qualifications* The ability to teach other courses listed in the Anthropology Programs Catalog: https://catalog.umd.umich.edu/undergraduate/coursesaz/anth/ Prior experience teaching related courses is strongly preferred. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States. PLEASE NOTE: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 285247 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - ANTHROPOLOGY Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Sociology & Anthropol Posting Begin/End Date 11/04/2026 - 11/14/2026 Salary $25,500.00 - $25,500.00 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/285119": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I-AIS",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I-AIS | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I-AIS Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Interested candidates should send a letter of application, current vita, and evidence of teaching effectiveness to: Jaclyn Guminski, Accounting and Finance Department, College of Business, University of Michigan-Dearborn, 114-A Fairlane Center South, 19000 Hubbard Drive, Dearborn, MI 48126-2638. Prior to being considered for an interview, three letters of recommendation will be required. Electronic applications are strongly encouraged and may be sent as a single PDF file to Jaclyn Guminski at [email protected] . Applications will be reviewed commencing immediately and will be accepted until the position is filled. Applications should be submitted through the University careers web site https://careers.umich.edu/ Job Summary The College of Business at the University of Michigan-Dearborn is seeking applications for multiple positions as an adjunct lecturer in Accounting, starting January 2027. The position will be responsible for teaching courses in Accounting Information Systems. Expertise in using Microsoft Access, QuickBooks Online, and Microsoft Excel software is required, as well as the willingness to learn a simple coding language used for grading. Expertise with or willingness to learn low-code/no-code business automation platforms such as Make is also required. The ability to teach both in-person and online or a hybrid using traditional materials and experiential-oriented practice sets is required, along with being open to try new approaches. Being able to do so at the graduate level is preferred but not required. The assigned effort will be 16 2/3% effort. The selected candidate is expected to begin work in Winter 2027. Interviews and the selection process will take place November 16-20, 2026. Provided a qualified candidate is identified, an offer is expected to be extended by November 30, 2026. Required Qualifications* Successful candidates must have a graduate university degree or an undergraduate university degree plus a professional accounting designation (e.g., CPA or CMA, or others), and at least three years of recent professional experience, preferably in a technology-intensive setting. Other professional qualifications and previous experience teaching accounting at the university level are also highly valued. Teaching will include night classes but the ability to teach during the day twice per week is also highly valued. This could be delivered in person, online synchronously, or asynchronously, depending on schedule requirements. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 285119 Working Title LEO Lecturer I-AIS Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Business Department Dbn Col of Bus-Acctg & Finance Posting Begin/End Date 11/02/2026 - 11/16/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/285328": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Analytical Chemistry",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Analytical Chemistry | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor of Analytical Chemistry Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please submit a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, teaching statement, research statement that includes plans for student involvement, an optional Holistic Engagement Statement, and the names and contact information of three professional references through Interfolio . Review of applications will begin on November 12, 2026 and continue until the position is filled; an offer of appointment will be made by April 1, 2027. The anticipated appointment beginning date is August 30,2027. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. All applications must be submitted via Interfolio; applications submitted through careers.umich.edu will not be reviewed. Who We Are The University of Michigan-Dearborn was founded in 1959 on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate in Dearborn, Michigan. As one of the University of Michigans three campuses, we serve over 8,000 students and provide high-quality undergraduate, graduate, professional, and continuing education programs that embody the prestige of the University of Michigan name. Our mission is rooted in a commitment to Metro Detroits well-being and diversity, fostering a caring, dynamic, intellectual, and socially welcoming environment. UM-Dearborn utilizes the Boyer Model of academic scholarship. We distinguish ourselves by building and nurturing intentional, mutually beneficial partnerships with local industries, governments, and community organizations. Our faculty are recognized for their excellence in scholarship and teaching and are actively engaged in professional and academic service. UM-Dearborn offers ample opportunities for creative, collaborative research, practice-based learning, and community engagement through scholarly and educational partnerships. Faculty have the privilege of working closely with their students, 47% of whom are first-generation college students, 46% are Pell Grant-eligible, and 31% are students of color. The average class size is 25 students, and 69% of classes have fewer than 30 students. U.S. News & World Report consistently ranks UM-Dearborn among the best regional universities in the Midwest. In 2024, UM-Dearborn ranked the number-one regional public university in Michigan and third in the Midwest. The university is also recognized on Forbes list of Americas Top Colleges. For more information, please visit our website at www.umdearborn.edu. Dearborn is a vibrant, diverse community centrally located within one of America?s largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Job Summary The Department of Natural Sciences in the College of Arts, Sciences, and Letters (CASL) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Analytical Chemistry, with a preferred starting date of August 30, 2027. We seek a colleague who will develop a productive, externally funded research program, provide excellent teaching, and mentor undergraduate students. Our multidisciplinary department offers programs in chemistry, biochemistry, biological sciences, environmental science, physics, and related fields. As an R2 research institution, UM-Dearborn values the scholar-teacher model, research-informed education, and undergraduate research experiences. The successful candidate will play an important role in strengthening the Analytical Chemistry and Instrumental Analysis area, including laboratory and instrumentation-based education, while supporting the continued development of its American Chemical Society (ACS)-approved chemistry program. Research specialties within analytical chemistry are open, and may include spectroscopy, electrochemistry, separations, mass spectrometry, chemical sensing, bioanalytical or environmental chemistry, materials characterization, and emerging data-intensive methods. The successful candidate will be expected to seek external funding, publish peer-reviewed articles, present research at national conferences, and mentor students in research. The University highly values research involving both undergraduate and graduate students. Laboratory facilities and competitive start-up funding will be provided, and interdisciplinary collaboration is strongly encouraged. The teaching load is 2:2 (two courses per semester), with opportunities to teach general chemistry, analytical chemistry, instrumental analysis, associated laboratories courses, and other undergraduate or graduate courses aligned with the candidates expertise and departmental needs. Faculty are also expected to contribute to departmental, university, and professional service and to foster an inclusive, collegial, and collaborative academic environment. This is an on-site position and expected to teach in various modalities. Required Qualifications* A Ph.D. in analytical chemistry, demonstrated scholarly productivity and the potential to establish an independent, externally funded research program; the potential for effective undergraduate teaching in analytical chemistry and instrumental analysis; and a commitment to student research mentorship, collegiality, and service. Desired Qualifications* Postdoctoral or equivalent research experience; expertise with modern analytical instrumentation; potential for interdisciplinary research and collaboration; undergraduate or graduate mentoring experience; and evidence of effective, and inclusive teaching. Why Work at U-M Dearborn? The University of Michigan-Dearborn is a vibrant and diverse community, centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, the University of Michigan-Dearborn offers a comprehensive benefits package, effective on your first day to help you and your family stay well, and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: Generous time off A retirement plan that provides two-for-one matching contributions after 12 months of eligible service Many choices for comprehensive health insurance, along with prescription drug plan Life Insurance Long-term disability coverage Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Eligibility for benefits is based on your job group, your appointment percentage and the length of your appointment. Learn more about employee benefits and eligibility from the UM Human Resources Benefits and Wellness webpage . Additional UM-Dearborn benefits include: Employees may be eligible for tuition reimbursement. Learn more about the tuition reimbursement policy . Eligible UM-Dearborn dependents may receive a 50% tuition and fees scholarship towards undergraduate courses. Learn more about the Dependent Tuition Scholarship from the Office of Financial Aid & Scholarships webpage . No charge for all faculty and staff parking permits. Learn more from the Parking Transportation webpage . Information about our mission, values and leadership can be found at: https://umdearborn.edu/about-um-dearborn/mission Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States. PLEASE NOTE: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 285328 Working Title Assistant Professor of Analytical Chemistry Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Physical Sciences Posting Begin/End Date 11/06/2026 - 2/02/2027 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/285323": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Medicinal Chemistry or Pharmacology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Medicinal Chemistry or Pharmacology | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor in Medicinal Chemistry or Pharmacology Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy (up to 2 pages), research plan (up to 5 pages), and contact information for three references through Interfolio : Teaching and research statements should address how you promote diversity of thought, perspective, background, and experience in your professional work. Review of applications will begin November12, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The anticipated appointment beginning date is August 30, 2027. All applications must be submitted via Interfolio; applications submitted through careers.umich.edu will not be reviewed. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States. The University of Michigan-Dearborn is unable to provide immigration-related sponsorship for this position. Who We Are The University of Michigan-Dearborn was founded in 1959 on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate in Dearborn, Michigan. As one of the University of Michigan's three campuses, we serve over 8,000 students and provide high-quality undergraduate, graduate, professional, and continuing education programs that embody the prestige of the University of Michigan name. Our mission is rooted in a commitment to Metro Detroit's well-being and diversity, fostering a caring, dynamic, intellectual, and socially welcoming environment. UM-Dearborn utilizes the Boyer Model of academic scholarship. We distinguish ourselves by building and nurturing intentional, mutually beneficial partnerships with local industries, governments, and community organizations. Our faculty are recognized for their excellence in scholarship and teaching and are actively engaged in professional and academic service. UM-Dearborn offers ample opportunities for creative, collaborative research, practice-based learning, and community engagement through scholarly and educational partnerships. Faculty have the privilege of working closely with their students, 47% of whom are first-generation college students, 46% are Pell Grant-eligible, and 31% are students of color. The average class size is 25 students, and 69% of classes have fewer than 30 students. U.S. News & World Report consistently ranks UM-Dearborn among the best regional universities in the Midwest. In 2024, UM-Dearborn ranked the number-one regional public university in Michigan and third in the Midwest. The university is also recognized on Forbes list of America's Top Colleges. For more information, please visit our website at www.umdearborn.edu . Dearborn is a vibrant, diverse community centrally located within one of America?s largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Job Summary The Department of Natural Sciences in the College of Arts, Sciences, and Letters (CASL) at the University of Michigan-Dearborn invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Chemistry, with expertise in Medicinal Chemistry, Chemical Biology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Pharmacology, or a closely related field. We seek an experimental scientist with a strong record of scholarly productivity and a demonstrated commitment to mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, particularly those pursuing pre-health and biomedical sciences. The preferred starting date is August 30, 2027. The Department of Natural Sciences is a collaborative community serving more than 600 majors in Chemistry, Biochemistry, and Biological Sciences, with a strong focus on pre-health and biomedical sciences. We value high-impact, research-informed teaching and meaningful research experiences for a diverse student body, including first-generation and Pell-eligible students. Opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration exist, including with programs in bioengineering. The successful candidate is expected to establish and maintain a sustainable, externally funded research program that includes peer-reviewed publications and active engagement of undergraduate and graduate students. The department encourages interdisciplinary research collaboration and student mentorship. The teaching load is 2:2 (two courses per semester), including organic chemistry and graduate-level courses such as Biochemical Pharmacology. Development of new courses in areas such as Molecular Targets, Drug Discovery, or Drug Development is encouraged. Faculty are expected to contribute to departmental and university service and to support an inclusive and collaborative academic environment. This is an on-site position and expected to teach in various modalities. Required Qualifications* Ph.D. in Organic Chemistry, Medicinal Chemistry, Pharmaceutical Sciences, or a closely related field by the start date Postdoctoral or equivalent research experience Evidence of scholarly productivity and potential to secure external funding Demonstrated commitment to effective, inclusive teaching and student mentoring at the undergraduate level Desired Qualifications* Experience in computational drug discovery or integrative methods that complement experimental research Experience with interdisciplinary collaborations Experience in new course development Why Work at U-M Dearborn? The University of Michigan-Dearborn is a vibrant and diverse community, centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, the University of Michigan-Dearborn offers a comprehensive benefits package, effective on your first day to help you and your family stay well, and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: Generous time off A retirement plan that provides two-for-one matching contributions after 12 months of eligible service Many choices for comprehensive health insurance, along with prescription drug plan Life Insurance Long-term disability coverage Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Eligibility for benefits is based on your job group, your appointment percentage and the length of your appointment. Learn more about employee benefits and eligibility from the UM Human Resources Benefits and Wellness webpage . Additional UM-Dearborn benefits include: Employees may be eligible for tuition reimbursement. Learn more about the tuition reimbursement policy . Eligible UM-Dearborn dependents may receive a 50% tuition and fees scholarship towards undergraduate courses. Learn more about the Dependent Tuition Scholarship from the Office of Financial Aid & Scholarships webpage . No charge for all faculty and staff parking permits. Learn more from the Parking Transportation webpage . Information about our mission, values and leadership can be found at: https://umdearborn.edu/about-um-dearborn/mission Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States. PLEASE NOTE: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 285323 Working Title Assistant Professor in Medicinal Chemistry or Pharmacology Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Physical Sciences Posting Begin/End Date 11/06/2026 - 2/02/2027 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/285444": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer III - BioChemistry",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer III - BioChemistry | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer III - BioChemistry Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should submit a cover letter and current CV (pdf), a statement of teaching philosophy, copies of teaching evaluations, and the names of three (3) references. Review of applications will begin November 9, 2026. The application deadline is December 7, 2026. An offer of appointment is expected to be made by April 1, 2027. Please submit your materials to Interfolio using this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/196922 . Applications submitted through careers.umich.edu will not be reviewed. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Selection Criteria: Applications will be evaluated based on relevant college-level teaching experience, demonstrated teaching effectiveness, experience with biochemistry laboratory instruction, student learning assessment, and development of laboratory exercises. Who We Are The University of Michigan-Dearborn (UM-Dearborn) is one of the three campuses of the University of Michigan. UM-Dearborn, a comprehensive university offering high-quality undergraduate, graduate, professional and continuing education to residents of southeastern Michigan, and attracts more than 9,000 students. Our faculty come from respected universities and doctoral programs, are recognized for excellence in research and teaching, and are active in professional and academic service roles in their respective fields. US News and World Report recently recognized our campus as a Best Regional University. The campus is located on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate. Dearborn is centrally located within one of Americas largest business regions. The geographically diverse area provides faculty with a variety of urban, suburban, and rural areas within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, Detroit suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Job Summary The Department of Natural Sciences at the University of Michigan-Dearborn invites applications for a full-time Laboratory Coordinator and LEO Lecturer III in Biochemistry, beginning August 30, 2027. This 100% appointment will cover the fall and winter semesters and will initially be for a three-year term, with the possibility of renewal based on successful performance reviews and departmental needs. The position combines teaching and laboratory coordination responsibilities. Teaching assignments will include approximately five courses per academic year, and may include both lower- and upper-level biochemistry lectures and laboratory courses. Opportunities to develop new courses may also arise. Service responsibilities are expected and may include departmental committee work, student advising, laboratory coordination, and other activities that support the departments academic mission. This position does not include a research component. Summer teaching opportunities may be available for additional compensation. Anticipated first year courses may include a combination of the following courses: BCHM 210 {Biochem Lab Techniques}, 4 credits, 33.33% Effort BCHM 480 {Biochemical Pharmacology}, 4 credits, 33.33% Effort BCHM 471/CHEM 471/BIOL 471 {Biochemistry II}, 4 credits, 33.33% Effort BCMH 473L/BIOL 473/CHEM 473 {Biochemistry II Laboratory} 4 credits, 33.33% Effort Course assignments and teaching responsibilities will be determined based on departmental needs. Required Qualifications* M.S. or Ph.D. in Biochemistry, chemistry education, or a closely related field. College-level teaching experience, preferably in biochemistry courses including those with laboratories. Desired Qualifications* Preference will be given to candidates who have advanced teaching experience and have participated in course assessment of student learning and development of innovative laboratory exercises. Why Work at U-M Dearborn? The University of Michigan-Dearborn is a vibrant and diverse community, centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, the University of Michigan-Dearborn offers a comprehensive benefits package, effective on your first day to help you and your family stay well, and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: Generous time off A retirement plan that provides two-for-one matching contributions after 12 months of eligible service Many choices for comprehensive health insurance, along with prescription drug plan Life Insurance Long-term disability coverage Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Eligibility for benefits is based on your job group, your appointment percentage and the length of your appointment. Learn more about employee benefits and eligibility from the UM Human Resources Benefits and Wellness webpage . Additional UM-Dearborn benefits include: Employees may be eligible for tuition reimbursement. Learn more about the tuition reimbursement policy . Eligible UM-Dearborn dependents may receive a 50% tuition and fees scholarship towards undergraduate courses. Learn more about the Dependent Tuition Scholarship from the Office of Financial Aid & Scholarships webpage . No charge for all faculty and staff parking permits. Learn more from the Parking Transportation webpage . Information about our mission, values and leadership can be found at: https://umdearborn.edu/about-um-dearborn/mission Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States. PLEASE NOTE: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 285444 Working Title LEO Lecturer III - BioChemistry Job Title LEO Lecturer III Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Physical Sciences Posting Begin/End Date 11/06/2026 - 11/27/2026 Salary $53,000.00 - $53,000.00 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    }
